{"name":"Dunnock Egg","hex":"#d9ece6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dunnock","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small European and Asian passerine bird, Prunella modularis; the hedge sparrow or hedge warbler."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dunnock"},{"term":"egg","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An approximately spherical or ellipsoidal body produced by birds, insects, reptiles, and other animals, often housing an embryo within a membrane or shell during its development.\n\n  The (usually) non-developing, edible egg (sense 1.1) of a domestic fowl such as a duck, goose, or, especially, chicken;  the contents of such an egg or eggs used as food.","An approximately spherical or ellipsoidal body produced by birds, insects, reptiles, and other animals, often housing an embryo within a membrane or shell during its development.\n\n  The (usually) non-developing, edible egg (sense 1.1) of a domestic fowl such as a duck, goose, or, especially, chicken;  the contents of such an egg or eggs used as food."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To throw (especially rotten) eggs (noun sense 1.1.1) at (someone or something).","To throw (especially rotten) eggs (noun sense 1.1.1) at (someone or something)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/egg"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dunnock","blocks":[{"text":"The dunnock is a small passerine, or perching bird, found throughout temperate Europe and into Asian Russia. Dunnocks have also been introduced and become common in New Zealand. It is the most widespread member of the accentor family; most other accentors are limited to mountain habitats. Other, largely archaic, English names for the dunnock include hedge accentor, hedge sparrow, hedge warbler, and titling."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Dunnock_%28Prunella_modularis%29_3.jpg/330px-Dunnock_%28Prunella_modularis%29_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunnock"},{"title":"Egg","blocks":[{"text":"An egg is an organic vessel grown by an animal to carry a possibly fertilized egg cell – a zygote. Within the vessel, an embryo is incubated until it has developed enough to survive on its own, at which point the animal hatches. Reproductive structures similar to the egg in other kingdoms are termed \"spores\", or in spermatophytes \"seeds\", or in gametophytes \"egg cells\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg/330px-Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egg"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/PrunellaModularisIUCN.png/500px-PrunellaModularisIUCN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/PrunellaModularisIUCN.png/1280px-PrunellaModularisIUCN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/PrunellaModularisIUCN.png/500px-PrunellaModularisIUCN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APrunellaModularisIUCN.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Prunella_modularis_-_Da%C4%9F_b%C3%BClb%C3%BCl%C3%BC_01-1.jpg/500px-Prunella_modularis_-_Da%C4%9F_b%C3%BClb%C3%BCl%C3%BC_01-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Prunella_modularis_-_Da%C4%9F_b%C3%BClb%C3%BCl%C3%BC_01-1.jpg/500px-Prunella_modularis_-_Da%C4%9F_b%C3%BClb%C3%BCl%C3%BC_01-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"P. m. obscura, with its browner head at all ages, is 